 👋 Hello, Saturday! In this special AM takeover, we explore the identity crisis facing the bachelor's degree as millions of students head back to campus.

 Thank you to Natalie Daher for orchestrating; editors Kate Marino, Ben Berkowitz and Carlos Cunha; and our Visuals dream team, Erin Davis and Sarah Grillo. Smart Brevity™ count: 1,374 words ... 5 mins. 🖊 What are your hopes and dreams for college in 2026? Whether you're a student, parent or professor, write us at finishline@axios.com to be featured in a future newsletter.

 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 1 big thing: New college doubts 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Stock: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 College was once sold as a ticket to the American dream.

 But students today are taking a tougher look at the college tradeoff: burdensome debt and no guarantee of a dream career — or employment at all , as AI threatens the jobs that the degrees were meant to unlock, Axios' Avery Lotz reports. "People didn't ask whether college mattered 20 years ago," says Courtney Brown, VP of impact and planning at Lumina Foundation. 

 Now, they're asking : "Are today's students going to be prepared for tomorrow's jobs? Is it really helping them out?" 📉 By the numbers : Confidence in higher education has slipped this year among both parties and all levels of educational attainment, according to Gallup . 

 Job market fears are also surging: Gallup found last year that just 19% of those with a college degree or more said it was a good time to find a quality job at the end of 2025, compared to 35% of those without one. But there's a paradox: Even as Americans sour on higher education, they keep sending their kids to campus.

 "There is still massive pressure on them from their high schools, the educators at their schools, their parents, their peers, and just the broader culture to still go to college," says The Up &amp; Up founder and Gen Z researcher Rachel Janfaza. Today's doubts fall into three buckets — in students' own words:

 💵 Affordability: "I didn't want to take out loans, and I didn't want to put my family through that stress of trying to fund it." —Gabriel Sheahan, a 24-year-old studying welding and fabrication at Lincoln Technical Institute, a trade school in New Jersey. 🏛️ Getting your money's worth: "How does it make sense that you're giving me this deadline to have my degree finished, and I can't even get the classes that I want? ... It feels ... like a rat race." —Rachel Sukkar, a 19-year-old philosophy major at UNC Chapel Hill. 🎓 Outcomes: "There's just so much volatility, and you can't guarantee the same amount of success that you could 20 years ago with a college degree now." —Moksha Agrawal, a 17-year-old incoming high school senior. Share this story . 

 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 2. 💰 Charted: Alumni earnings premium 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Data : Economic Policy Institute. Chart: Erin Davis/Axios Visuals The boost that college graduates get in pay over those who don't go to college is huge — but it's been declining a bit in recent years, Axios Markets co-author Emily Peck writes. 

 Last year , the average college graduate earned 55.2% more than the typical high school grad, per Labor Department data analyzed by the progressive Economic Policy Institute. That's down from a peak of more than 60% a decade ago. 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 3. 🔄 Colleges race to be AI-forward 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Stock: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 AI is forcing colleges to rethink what students need to learn — and what a degree should prepare them to do, Axios' Megan Morrone and Herb Scribner write. 

 Why it matters: Employers increasingly expect new graduates to arrive with AI fluency, even as colleges are still figuring out how to teach it. 💻 Split screen: This year's graduating high school seniors have had access to ChatGPT since freshman year, while this year's graduating college students booed mentions of AI at commencement.

 As students' command of AI has improved, colleges have revisited whether using it should always count as cheating. "Now anyone can get that expertise in a minute," OpenAI's VP for education, Leah Belsky, tells Axios. Higher education "is more coaching, mentoring, guiding students" through prompts and analyzing outputs, she says. 📈 The pressure is growing: The number of employers that expect entry-level workers to be AI-competent has nearly tripled since last fall, according to the National Association of Colleges and Employers.

 🦾 Zoom out: Schools are racing to catch up. American , Purdue and Miami University in Ohio have launched AI-forward initiatives.

 🎟️ Another route: Short-term certificate programs are becoming a faster, cheaper way to build AI skills — and a way for universities to reach a broader pool of students, Bloomberg reports ( gift link ).

 In 2022, just 2% of the professional certificate market focused on AI. This year, short-term AI training programs take up nearly a third. 📚 What students are saying: UC Davis student Burton Baecker told Axios he added finance to his accounting studies after seeing how quickly AI could perform accounting tasks.

 What to watch: As AI tools take on more entry-level tasks, employers are raising the bar for young workers.

 New graduates are now expected to manage projects and AI agents, conduct deeper research, present findings and defend their work, Casey Evans, interim dean at American University's Kogod School of Business, tells Axios. Share this story . 

 
 
