 4. ✈️ America&#x27;s global student edge is slipping 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Data: Open Doors, IIE. Chart: Erin Davis/Axios Visuals America is losing one of its quietest superpowers: its ability to attract the world's most ambitious students, who also provide a crucial revenue stream for universities, Axios' Russell Contreras reports. 

 By the numbers: There was a 17% drop in new international student enrollment for fall 2025, recent survey findings by the Association of International Educators reveal.

 This translates to over $1.1 billion in lost revenue for the U.S. economy and nearly 23,000 fewer jobs. 🌎 Between the lines: The plunge isn't just about strict immigration policies or visa backlogs from the Trump administration. A broader, unwelcoming national climate is deterring students.

 That reputation is prompting foreign talent to explore other options before they even apply here, Andrew Gillen, a research fellow at the Cato Institute, tells Axios. He compares turning away global talent to banning left-handed people from school: "You're sort of closing off access to whatever they would have been able to accomplish." Share this story . 

 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 5. 🔨 Gen Z nails its AI backup plan 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Stock: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Some young workers are swapping laptops for hard hats, betting that a welding torch or a tool belt could offer more protection from AI disruption, Axios' Josephine Walker writes. 

 🧰 Why it matters: More young Americans are looking to the skilled trades for lower debt, stable pay and work that's harder to automate.

 John Zink, vice president at the PHCC Educational Foundation, an apprentice training group, puts it bluntly: "We have the high-demand jobs that AI can't take because AI can't run pipe or install HVAC systems." 💬 Octavius Jackson, 25 , spent two years at Prairie View A&amp;M before pivoting to the Aviation Institute of Maintenance for an airframe and power plant certificate.

 He said choosing a career less vulnerable to AI factored into his decision. "You don't want a robot fixing up these planes," he told Axios. 🛠️ What to watch : High schools are expanding programs that give students a direct path into skilled work.

 Case in point: Philadelphia's Father Judge High School graduated 24 students from its welding course last year. All got jobs paying $50,000+ a year. Go deeper . 

 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 6. 🚪 Sorry, we&#x27;re closed 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Data: BestColleges. Map: Erin Davis/Axios Visuals The pandemic accelerated a higher-ed shakeout that's still forcing colleges to close, merge or reinvent themselves.

 Since March 2020, at least 49 nonprofit colleges have closed or announced closures. Forty others have merged — affecting more than 53,000 students, according to the higher-ed site BestColleges . Historically, fewer than half of students reenroll elsewhere .

 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 7. 🚫 Tech&#x27;s pitch to skip college 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Stock: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Some of the tech industry's most influential CEOs are challenging the four-year degree as the default path for elite talent — and building alternatives to it, Axios' Herb Scribner writes. 

 Palantir created the Meritocracy Fellowship , which urges high school grads to "skip the debt" and "skip the indoctrination" in favor of a "Palantir degree." Peter Thiel founded the two-year Thiel Fellowship , where participants skip or leave college. Share this story . 

 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 8. ⏱️ 1 for the road: Speedy degrees 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Stock: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Virginia and Ohio are developing 90-credit bachelor's degrees that students could finish in three years — joining a fast-growing national effort to rethink how long college should take.

 The two states hope to launch pilot programs in 2028, Axios Richmond's Karri Peifer reports . 💰 Colleges struggling to fill seats see the programs as a way to attract students put off by the cost and length of a traditional degree. 

